Consider a Laplace-distributed random variable $X$ with unknown parameters $\mu_0 \in \R$ and $\sigma_0 > 0$. In other words: $X\sim\text{LP}(\mu_0,\sigma_0)$ with the following density function:

$$ p(x) = \frac{1}{2\sigma_0}\,\exp\left(-\frac{|x-\mu_0|}{\sigma_0}\right) $$

Unfortunately, the model is misspecified and $X$ is assumed to be normally distributed with a set of parameters $\thetav = (\mu, \sigma^2)$, meaning that $X \sim \normal (\mu,\sigma^2) $

$$ q(x|\thetav) = \frac{1}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi}} \exp \left(-\frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{x-\mu}{\sigma} \right)^2 \right) $$

%\begin{enumerate}
%  \item
    Calculate the set of parameters $\thetav$ that minimizes the Kullback-Leibler Divergence $D_{KL}(p \| q_{\thetav})$.
    
    
    \emph{Hint}: Use the fact that for  $X \sim \text{LP}(\mu_0,\sigma_0)$, the following properties apply: $\E(X  )=\mu_0$ and $\var(X)=2 \sigma_0^2$.

%\end{enumerate}

